ite40†References L51: cite41†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L52: \nL53: arXiv:2609.06175v1 [math.CO] 05 Sep 2026\nL54: # Sunflower-Free Uniform Families: Recursive Constructions and Explicit Bounds\nL55: \nL56: Edward Axante ^{†}^{†}thanks: Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science, University of Bucharest, Romania.    Cristian Budala^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1    David Chitic^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1    Bogdan Dumitru^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1    and Mihai Nacu^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1\nL57: ###### Abstract\nL58: Let $f(w,k)$ be the maximum size of a $w$-uniform family containing no sunflower with $k$ petals. We introduce a recursive construction for sunflower-free families and use it to obtain a general lower bound on the exponential growth rate of $f(w,k)$. We also prove a general upper bound for $3$-uniform families with at least four petals. Our results give $39\\leq f(3,4)\\leq 49$, $f(3,5)\\leq 146$, $153\\leq f(3,6)\\leq 255$, $259\\leq f(3,7)\\leq 474$, and $54\\leq f(4,3)\\leq 83$.\nL59: In addition, we prove that the maximum size of an intersecting $4$-uniform family containing no sunflower with three petals is $27$. The upper bounds $49$ and $83$ are computer-assisted. The finite lower bounds come from explicit constructions.\nL60: ## 1 Introduction\nL61: A sunflower with $k$ petals, or $k$-sunflower, is a collection of $k$ distinct sets whose pairwise intersections are equal. Their common intersection is called the core and may be empty. Let $f(w,k)$ denote the maximum size of a family of distinct $w$-element sets containing no $k$-sunflower. The sunflower conjecture of Erdős and Rado asks whether, for every fixed $k$, there is a constant $C_{k}$ such that $f(w,k)\\leq C_{k}^{w}$ for every $w$ [cite42†11 ].\nL139: ### 2.3 The graph case\nL140: \nL141: For integers $D,q\\geq 1$, let $\\operatorname{CH}(D,q)$ be the maximum number of edges in a graph with maximum degree at most $D$ and matching number at most $q$. The Chvátal–Hanson theorem [cite48†9 ] gives\nL142: \nL143:  | $$\\operatorname{CH}(D,q)=Dq+\\left\\lfloor\\frac{D}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\left\\lfloor\\frac{q}{\\lceil D/2\\rceil}\\right\\rfloor.$$  |  | (2.5)\nL144: \nL349: For comparison, Abbott, Hanson, and Sauer proved $B(3)\\geq\\sqrt{10}$ [cite50†3 ]. This is stronger than the $k=3$ specialization of cite70†theorem 3.5 . Their stronger three-petal estimate comes from a substitution construction whose proof is specific to three petals, whereas cite70†theorem 3.5 applies to every $k\\geq 3$. The constructions of Abbott and Exoo imply\nL350: \nL351:  | $$B(4)\\geq 38^{1/3},\\qquad B(5)\\geq\\sqrt{20},\\qquad B(6)\\geq 146^{1/3}$$  |\nL352: [cite46†5 ]. The three corresponding bounds in cite71†corollary 3.6 are larger.\nL353: \nL354: The exponential-rate argument uses cite64†equation 3.3 . The other matching caps in cite72†theorem 3.1 give additional finite recurrences. Appendix cite39†A records the alternative capacity recurrence.\nL420: By cite75†lemmas 2.2 and cite76†2.3 , every point has degree at most $h_{k}$. Counting incidences between the members of $\\mathcal{F}$ and the $3v$ points of $T$ gives\nL421: \nL422:  | $$t_{1}+2t_{2}+3t_{3}\\leq 3vh_{k}.$$  |  | (4.3)\nL423: \nL424: The matching members themselves give\nL425: \nL426:  | $$t_{3}\\geq v.$$  |  | (4.4)\nL427: \nL428: Fix $i$ and write $M_{i}=\\{a,b,c\\}$. On the points outside $T$, define\nL429: \nL430:  | $$S_{a}=\\{F\\setminus\\{a\\}:F\\in\\mathcal{F},\\ F\\cap T=\\{a\\}\\},$$  |\nL448: ### 4.3 Numerical consequences\nL449: \nL450: For $k\\geq 5$, one has $\\operatorname{CH}(k-1,k-2)\\geq(k-1)(k-2)\\geq 3(k-1)$, so $c_{k}=\\operatorname{CH}(k-1,k-2)$. cite77†Lemma 4.1 controls the case of two or three nonempty residue graphs. At $k=4$, it gives $c_{4}=9$, and cite81†theorem 4.2 yields $f(3,4)\\leq 57$.\nL451: \nL452: ###### Corollary 4.3.\nL453: \nL454: The general bound gives\nL455: \nL456:  | $$f(3,5)\\leq 146,\\qquad f(3,6)\\leq 255,\\qquad f(3,7)\\leq 474.$$  |\nL484: Put $\\mathcal{F}_{39}=\\mathcal{G}\\cup\\mathcal{P}\\cup\\mathcal{Q}$. These three families are disjoint and\nL485: \nL486:  | $$|\\mathcal{F}_{39}|=3+3\\binom{3}{2}+3\\cdot 3^{2}=39.$$  |\nL487: \nL488: We exclude the three possible core sizes. Every pair has degree at most $3$. The pairs $\\{\\ell_{ij},x\\}$ with $x\\in V_{i}\\cup V_{j}$ have degree $3$; all other pair types have degree at most $2$. Thus there is no $4$-sunflower with a two-point core.\nL523: ###### Lemma 5.4 (Rooted blocks).\nL524: \nL525: Every intersecting, $3$-uniform family without a $4$-sunflower has at most $10$ members [cite47†13 , Proposition 6.3]. The rooted isomorphism-class counts used below are\nL526: \nL527: rooted block list  | classes\nL528: --- | ---\nL529: $|\\mathcal{A}|=8$ and $p(\\mathcal{A},T)=0$  | $45$\nL530: $|\\mathcal{A}|=9$ and $p(\\mathcal{A},T)=0$  | $27$\nL531: all rooted blocks with $|\\mathcal{A}|=9$  | $665$\nL532: all rooted blocks with $|\\mathcal{A}|=10$  | $114$\nL577: If $y_{2}\\neq y_{3}$, the two corresponding edges in $\\operatorname{Lk}_{\\mathcal{F}}(s_{1})$ are disjoint. Deleting $y_{2},y_{3}$ from $L^{\\prime}_{s_{1}}(S)\\cong R$ must then destroy every two-matching. If, say, $y_{2}$ were not a vertex of $R$, deleting $y_{2},y_{3}$ would remove at most one vertex of $R$, and the remaining graph would still contain a two-matching. Hence both $y_{2}$ and $y_{3}$ belong to $R$.\nL580: ### 5.3 The maximum-matching decomposition\nL581: \nL582: ###### Lemma 5.6.\nL583: \nL584: If $\\mathcal{F}$ is $3$-uniform, has no $4$-sunflower, and $\\nu(\\mathcal{F})\\leq 2$, then $|\\mathcal{F}|\\leq 35$. Equivalently,\nL585: \nL586:  | $$\\psi(3,4,3)\\leq 35.$$  |\nL795: For $\\{i,j,k\\}=\\{1,2,3\\}$, the subfamily $\\mathcal{F}_{j}\\cup\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\cup\\mathcal{C}_{i}$ has matching number exactly $2$. It contains $T_{j},T_{k}$, while three disjoint members together with $T_{i}$ would form a four-matching. Thus $\\mathcal{C}_{i}$ is a cross class to which cite95†lemma 5.9 applies.\nL822: This section proves $54\\leq f(4,3)\\leq 83$ and determines the intersecting extremum $\\psi(4,3,2)=27$ exactly. For the upper bound, we fix a member $R$ and split the family into the members meeting $R$ and the members disjoint from $R$. The members disjoint from $R$ pairwise intersect, so their number is at most $\\psi(4,3,2)$. The members meeting $R$ number at most $56$, by an analysis of the exact trace classes of $R$. Together these bounds give $f(4,3)\\leq 56+27=83$.\nL868: ###### Lemma 6.2 (Exact-trace residues).\nL869: \nL870: The residues $F\\setminus T$, $F\\in\\mathcal{C}_{T}(R)$, are pairwise distinct and form an intersecting, $(4-|T|)$-uniform family without a $3$-sunflower. Consequently,\nL871: \nL872:  | $$|\\mathcal{C}_{T}(R)|\\leq\\psi(4-|T|,3,2).$$  |  | (6.3)\nL899: Equality holds in both bounds. Thus $|U_{x}|=6$, every pair of support points has degree $2$, and every support point has degree $5$. In particular, $\\mathcal{H}_{x}$ is a simple $2$-$(6,3,2)$ design. Five blocks contain any fixed support point, and two blocks contain any fixed pair. Thus two of the ten blocks avoid any prescribed pair of support points. In particular, a block avoids any prescribed set of at most two support points.\nL900: ###### Lemma 6.3 (Incident pair traces).\nL901: \nL902: If $x$ is saturated, then $r_{x}\\leq 6$.\nL903: ###### Proof.\nL904: \nL905: For $y\\in R\\setminus\\{x\\}$, the residues of $\\mathcal{C}_{\\{x,y\\}}(R)$ are distinct pairwise-intersecting edges. If $p_{xy}=3$, they cannot form a three-edge star, since their lifts would form a sunflower. They therefore form a triangle, with support $V_{y}$.\nL906: \nL907: Consider the complete link at $x$. Every point in this triple family has degree at most $f(2,3)=6$. A point in $U_{x}\\cap V_{y}$ would occur five times in $\\mathcal{H}_{x}$ and twice in the $y$-triangle. Hence\nL920: For a saturated point, cite112†lemma 6.3 gives $r_{x}\\leq 6$; for every other point, cite113†equation 6.4 gives $r_{x}\\leq 9$. Since each pair trace is counted at both endpoints,\nL921: \nL922:  | $$2p=\\sum_{x\\in R}r_{x}\\leq 6\\sigma+9(4-\\sigma)\\leq 144-3s.$$  |  | (6.8)\nL923: \nL924: Substitute $s=h-p-t$ into cite114†equations 6.6 and cite115†6.8 . This gives\nL925: \nL926:  | $$p+2t\\leq 76-h,\\qquad p+3t\\geq 3h-144.$$  |\nL927: \nL928: Hence $t\\geq 4h-220$. Since $t\\leq 4$, one has $h\\leq 56$. Equality forces\nL929: \nL930:  | $$(s,p,t)=(40,12,4).$$  |  | (6.9)\nL954: Equality holds in both bounds. Thus the support has exactly six points, every pair of support points has degree $2$, and $\\mathcal{K}_{x}$ is a simple $2$-$(6,3,2)$ design.\nL955: \nL956: Fix $x$ and put $Y=R\\setminus\\{x\\}$. The design $\\mathcal{K}_{x}$ contains\nL957: \nL958:  | $$Y,\\qquad B_{m}=(Y\\setminus\\{m\\})\\cup\\{z_{m}\\}\\quad(m\\in Y).$$  |\nL960: Write $Y=\\{y,a,b\\}$. The two design blocks through $y$ not displayed above come from $\\mathcal{C}_{\\{x,y\\}}(R)$. The pair $\\{y,z_{y}\\}$ occurs in none of the displayed blocks, so both new blocks contain $z_{y}$. The pairs $\\{y,z_{a}\\}$ and $\\{y,z_{b}\\}$ each occur once and each needs one further occurrence. Their residue edges are therefore\nL961: \nL962:  | $$\\bigl\\{\\{z_{y},z_{a}\\},\\{z_{y},z_{b}\\}\\bigr\\}.$$  |  | (6.11)\nL963: For any two indices in $R$, choose $x$ outside them. The preceding argument shows that their $z$-points are distinct. Thus all four points $z_{m}$ are distinct.\nL964: \nL965: Finally fix distinct $x,y$ and write $R\\setminus\\{x,y\\}=\\{a,b\\}$. The $x$-view of the exact pair class is cite117†equation 6.11 . Its $y$-view is\nL966: \nL967:  | $$\\bigl\\{\\{z_{x},z_{a}\\},\\{z_{x},z_{b}\\}\\bigr\\}.$$  |\nL968: \nL969: Both views describe\nL970: \nL971:  | $$\\{F\\setminus\\{x,y\\}:F\\in\\mathcal{C}_{\\{x,y\\}}(R)\\},$$  |\nL972: so they must be equal. They cannot be equal because the four $z$-points are distinct. ∎\nL973: ###### Proposition 6.5 (Meeting-neighborhood bound).\nL974: \nL975: For every $R\\in\\mathcal{F}$,\nL976: \nL977:  | $$|\\mathcal{N}_{\\mathcal{F}}[R]|\\leq 56.$$  |  | (6.12)\nL978: \nL979: ###### Proof.\nL980: \nL981: The preceding inequalities give $h\\leq 56$, and cite118†lemma 6.4 excludes equality. Thus $h\\leq 55$. Apply cite119†equation 6.5 . ∎\nL982: ### 6.3 The intersecting extremum\nL983: \nL984: To determine $\\psi(4,3,2)$, it remains to prove its upper bound. This follows from an exhaustive enumeration of the objects defined below.\nL985: \nL986: The decision problem asks whether there is a family with exactly $28$ distinct members, called rows below, satisfying the following conditions:\nL987: \nL988:   1. 1.\nL989: \nL990: every row has size four;\nL991: \nL992:   2. 2.\nL993: \nL994: every two rows intersect;\nL995: \nL996:   3. 3.\nL997: \nL998: no three rows have equal pairwise intersections.\nL999: The ground set is not fixed. If such a family existed, its support would have size at most\nL1000: \nL1001:  | $$4+27\\cdot 3=85,$$  |  | (6.13)\nL1002: \nL1003: because every row after the first meets the first row.\nL1004: A partial family is represented by its two-colored bipartite incidence graph. Row vertices and point vertices are the two color classes. The first row is fixed as $\\{0,1,2,3\\}$. If a parent uses the point labels $0,\\ldots,C-1$, a candidate row is generated by choosing an $s$-subset of the old labels, for $0\\leq s\\leq 4$, and adjoining the fresh consecutive labels\nL1005: \nL1006:  | $$C,C+1,\\ldots,C+(4-s)-1.$$  |\nL1007: \nL1008: This list contains every extension up to relabelling points not used by the parent.\nL1009: A candidate is rejected if it repeats a row, misses a prior row, or completes a $3$-sunflower. For prior rows $A,B$ and candidate $S$, the last test is\nL1010: \nL1011:  | $$A\\cap B\\subseteq S,\\qquad S\\cap A\\subseteq B,\\qquad S\\cap B\\subseteq A.$$  |\nL1012: \nL1013: These three inclusions are equivalent to $A\\cap B=A\\cap S=B\\cap S$. All rejection conditions are hereditary under row deletion.\nL1187: Some external results and analytic arguments used in the paper are outside the Lean formalisation. The Chvátal–Hanson upper bound is supplied as a hypothesis. For the three exponential-rate bounds, Lean verifies constructions of size at least $c\\,r_{k}^{w}$, for some $c>0$ and every $w\\geq 3$; the limiting and asymptotic arguments are not included. The exhaustive computations behind $f(3,4)\\leq 49$ and $f(4,3)\\leq 83$ are checked separately, as described in cite20†sections 5 and cite28†6 .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSunflower-Free Uniform Families: Recursive Constructions and Explicit Bounds (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06175v1)\nciteturn13view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn12view0\",\"pattern\":\"8 Formal verification\"}); Total lines: 1283\nL29:     3. cite19†4.3 Numerical consequences L30:   6. cite20†5 Bounds for $f(3,4)$ L31:     1. cite21†5.1 A family of 39 triples L32:     2. cite22†5.2 Local structure around a triple L33:     3. cite23†5.3 The maximum-matching decomposition L34:     4. cite24†5.4 Rigid blocks and finite profiles L35:     5. cite25†5.5 Cross-family bounds L36:     6. cite26†5.6 Exclusion of the profiles L37:     7. cite27†5.7 Computational verification L38:   7. cite28†6 Bounds for $f(4,3)$ L39:     1. cite29†6.1 Finite triple inputs L40:     2. cite30†6.2 The closed meeting neighborhood L41:     3. cite31†6.3 The intersecting extremum L42:     4. cite32†6.4 The global bounds L43:     5. cite33†6.5 Computational verification L44:   8. cite34†7 Lower bounds for six and seven petals L45:     1. cite35†7.1 Six petals L46:     2. cite36†7.2 Seven petals L47:     3. cite37†7.3 Verification of the finite witness L48:   9. cite38†8 Formal verification in Lean L49:   10. cite39†A Alternative capacity recurrence L50:   11. cite40†References L51: cite41†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L52: \nL53: arXiv:2609.06175v1 [math.CO] 05 Sep 2026\nL54: # Sunflower-Free Uniform Families: Recursive Constructions and Explicit Bounds\nL55: \nL56: Edward Axante ^{†}^{†}thanks: Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science, University of Bucharest, Romania.    Cristian Budala^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1    David Chitic^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1    Bogdan Dumitru^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1    and Mihai Nacu^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1\nL57: ###### Abstract\nL58: Let $f(w,k)$ be the maximum size of a $w$-uniform family containing no sunflower with $k$ petals. We introduce a recursive construction for sunflower-free families and use it to obtain a general lower bound on the exponential growth rate of $f(w,k)$. We also prove a general upper bound for $3$-uniform families with at least four petals. Our results give $39\\leq f(3,4)\\leq 49$, $f(3,5)\\leq 146$, $153\\leq f(3,6)\\leq 255$, $259\\leq f(3,7)\\leq 474$, and $54\\leq f(4,3)\\leq 83$.\nL1174: ###### Proof.\nL1175: \nL1176: The supports of $\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\mathcal{B}$ are disjoint. A sunflower with nonempty core cannot use triples from both families. An empty-core sunflower is a matching, while\nL1177: \nL1178:  | $$\\nu(\\mathcal{A}\\mathbin{\\dot{\\cup}}\\mathcal{B})=\\nu(\\mathcal{A})+\\nu(\\mathcal{B})\\leq 5+1=6.$$  |\nL1179: \nL1180: Thus $\\mathcal{A}\\mathbin{\\dot{\\cup}}\\mathcal{B}$ contains no $7$-sunflower and has $217+42=259$ members. The upper bound is cite129†corollary 4.3 . ∎\nL1181: ### 7.3 Verification of the finite witness\nL1182: \nL1183: The certificate for the lower bound $f(3,7)\\geq 259$ consists of the explicit $217$-triple family and the $42$-member family $\\mathcal{B}$ constructed above. The standalone verifier checks the size and distinctness of the $217$-triple family, its support, every pair degree, and every vertex-link matching number.\nL1184: ## 8 Formal verification in Lean\nL1185: The main combinatorial arguments of cite7†sections 2 , cite12†3 and cite16†4 , the appendix capacity recurrence, and every explicit finite witness used for the numerical lower bounds have been formalised in the Lean 4 proof assistant [cite132†10 ] over the Mathlib library [cite133†14 ]. Since the finiteness of $f(w,k)$ rests on the Erdős–Rado theorem, the formalisation represents $f$ and $\\psi$ by pairs of upper-bound and witness predicates.\nL1186: The finite witness families are encoded explicitly in Lean, and their properties are verified by computation within Lean’s kernel.\nL1187: Some external results and analytic arguments used in the paper are outside the Lean formalisation. The Chvátal–Hanson upper bound is supplied as a hypothesis. For the three exponential-rate bounds, Lean verifies constructions of size at least $c\\,r_{k}^{w}$, for some $c>0$ and every $w\\geq 3$; the limiting and asymptotic arguments are not included. The exhaustive computations behind $f(3,4)\\leq 49$ and $f(4,3)\\leq 83$ are checked separately, as described in cite20†sections 5 and cite28†6 .\nL1188: The correspondence between the paper statements and formal theorems, together with the pinned toolchain and build instructions, is part of the supplementary material.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA1K Agentic SuperMath · Transversal Six, Finite Obstructions and Boundary Profiles in Rank-Four Sunflower-Free Families (https://a1k.ai/agentic-math/rank-four-sunflower-free-families)\nciteturn13view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn12view1\",\"pattern\":\"85\"}); Total lines: 317\nL160: More generally, every distinct pair of codegree six in a rank-four sunflower-free family has an exact residual link consisting of the three edges of one triangle and the three edges of a disjoint second triangle. All six residual points lie outside the pair. Restoring the pair to each residual edge recovers precisely the six original members in its star. The declaration `pair_codegree_six_exact_two_triangles` preserves all distinctness and disjointness conditions.\nL161: It does not imply the existence of a global family satisfying an arbitrary collection of such local models.\nL162: ### 7. A finite and universe-independent route to I(27)\nL163: \nL164: Theorem T7. I(27) is equivalent to nonexistence of a 28-member intersecting sunflower-free family of four-element subsets of Fin 85. The counterexample reduction works for an ambient type in any universe.\nL165: \nL166: From any counterexample select exactly twenty-eight members H. Choose an anchor R∈H. Every other member meets R and therefore contributes at most three points outside R. Thus\nL167: \nL168: $$|\\operatorname{supp}(H)|\\le4+27\\cdot3=85. \\qquad\\text{(E5)}$$\nL169: The support argument in (E5) already appears in [2], §6.3, Eq. (6.13). The local contribution here is its checked use in a universe-independent finite-obstruction equivalence, including the preservation of the family predicates under transport.\nL170: Restrict H to its finite support, choose an equivalence from that support to a finite ordinal, and embed it into Fin 85. The transport preserves member cardinality, family cardinality, all intersections, intersectingness and sunflower-freeness. The converse specializes I(27) to Fin 85. The declaration `intersecting_upper_twenty_seven_iff_no_finite_obstruction` checks the equivalence. It is a reduction, not an exclusion result: the finite obstruction has not been ruled out.\nL171: There are 2,024,785 possible four-subsets of an 85-point ground set. A Boolean encoding may select exactly twenty-eight, forbid disjoint selected pairs, and forbid every three distinct selected sets with equal pairwise intersections, including empty core. A practical certificate route requires a proved correspondence between this Boolean predicate and the finite family predicate, a complete coverage argument, and a Lean-checked refutation or a proved sound certificate checker.\nL172: No satisfiability run or complexity guarantee is claimed.\nL173: ### 8. Three explicit open inputs and the residual profile constraints\nL174: Theorem T8. The three ordinary hypotheses I(27), ¬ProfileARealizable and ¬ProfileBRealizable imply G(80). The realizability predicates quantify over actual finite rank-four sunflower-free families with eighty-one members, support seventeen, no degree-seventeen point, the respective degree-count row, and at most one high point in each member. They are existential mathematical propositions, not project axioms. The checked terminal declaration is `unrestricted_eighty_of_three_open_inputs`.\nL175: The reduction diagram is: I(27) implies G(81); I(27) plus an 81-member family gives the literal A-or-B boundary; exclusion of both actual-family profiles removes equality eighty-one. None of the three inputs is discharged here.\nL176: The checked zero-high member-count theorem shows that removing the high star in Profile A leaves 61 members. Its twelve neighbors lose five incidences each; the remaining four nonhigh points lose none. The proposed residual census is twelve degrees 14 and four degrees 19, with 12·14+4·19=244=4·61. In Profile B the two high stars are disjoint because no member contains two high points; the checked zero-high member-count theorem shows that deleting them leaves 41 members.\nL187: There is also a distinct computational path toward I(27). T7 has completed the exact twenty-eight-member reduction and transport to Fin 85. The remaining computational steps are an equivalence with a search or satisfiability encoding, complete case coverage, and verifiable exclusion evidence. Symmetry restrictions must be justified: canonical augmentation or orbit pruning cannot become a theorem premise merely because a program implements it.\nL193: It also formalizes the universe-independent equivalence between I(27) and absence of a literal 28-member finite obstruction on 85 points, using the existing support argument cited in T7. The direct census and separate nonrealizability predicates make the remaining boundary obligations inspectable. These statements concern actual finite families and preserve every theorem-strength premise explicitly.\nL227: T6c  | Explicit nineteen-member construction  | `Erdos20V13Endpoint.nineteen_member_endpoint_counterexample`  | No\nL228: T6d, degree  | Intersecting rank 4, exactly 28 members, point degree 19  | `Erdos20V13Intersecting.intersecting_twenty_eight_degree_nineteen_partner_bounds`  | No\nL229: T6d, geometry  | Rank 4, distinct pair of codegree 6  | `Erdos20V13Intersecting.pair_codegree_six_exact_two_triangles`  | No\nL230: T7  | Exact finite obstruction predicate on Fin 85  | `Erdos20V13FiniteReduction.intersecting_upper_twenty_seven_iff_no_finite_obstruction`  | Equivalence, no assumed I(27)\nL231: T8  | Nonrealizability of both literal profiles  | `Erdos20V13Boundary.unrestricted_eighty_of_three_open_inputs`  | Yes\nL232: All family results also retain exact uniformity, distinct members, three distinct petals, and the empty-core convention. T4–T6 do not assume all-meeting54, |F|=81 or a fixed support. The graph input uses `SimpleGraph.colorable_of_cliqueFree_lt_minDegree` at the pinned mathlib revision; its strict threshold is retained. In the seventeen-link application n=17 and δ≥8 give 40>34; in the nineteen-link application n=19 gives 40>38.\nL233: The family-to-graph bridge supplies finite vertices, no loops and triangle-freeness. The separateness of coloring classes and support sets is maintained.\nL234: The ten-design terminology denotes an intersecting sunflower-free triple family of cardinality ten, with its proved isomorphism class on six supported points, degree five and pair codegree two. It is not an arbitrary numerical 2-design axiom. In six distinct coordinates a,b,c,z_a,z_b,z_c, the inherited `canonicalTen` consists of {a,b,c}, {b,c,z_a}, {a,c,z_b}, {a,b,z_c}, {a,z_a,z_b}, {a,z_a,z_c}, {b,z_b,z_a}, {b,z_b,z_c}, {c,z_c,z_a}, and {c,z_c,z_b}.\nL258: I(27)  | External computer-assisted claim; locally unresolved  | Equivalent here to absence of the Fin 85 obstruction\nL259: G(80)  | Locally unresolved  | Follows from the three inputs in T8\nL260: Nonrealizability of Profile A and Profile B  | Both unresolved  | Necessary degree rows are not realizability decisions\nL261: All-rank fixed-base conjecture  | Not proved by this development  | Fixed-rank structural progress does not settle it\n"
